# ===========================================================================
# kyl23-extra.rc -- board init for the 7.1 port of the Kyocera msm8926.
#
# This file is part of the device tree (system/etc/init/kyl23-extra.rc) and is
# what makes the ROM boot at all: the vendor blob set is 4.4, the framework is
# 7.1, and everything in between is bridged here or in the helpers it starts.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Wi-Fi: finished, and this is the shape it ended in.
#
# The device shipped a 4.4 wpa_supplicant (v2.1-devel) that speaks the old
# ctrl_iface dialect, so several rounds of work went into making a 7.1
# framework talk to it -- including a patch to /system/framework/wifi-service.jar
# that made WifiNative.listNetworks() drop the LAST_ID argument, because the
# 4.4 binary answered UNKNOWN COMMAND to it.
#
# That patch was the bug.  WifiConfigStore.loadNetworks() pages through the
# network list and stops when a page holds nothing but the header; with the
# argument gone, "page two" is page one again, the same configKey shows up
# twice, the duplicate branch calls removeNetwork() on the entry it just read,
# and the saved network is deleted on every Wi-Fi toggle.  Settings kept
# showing the AP, the supplicant no longer had it, every SET_NETWORK after that
# answered FAIL, and the next toggle wrote an empty config -- the password
# prompt.
#
# The fix is not another patch.  /system/bin/wpa_supplicant is now built from
# cm-14.1's own external/wpa_supplicant_8 (v2.6-devel), which pages correctly:
# LIST_NETWORKS LAST_ID=-1 returns everything, LAST_ID=<last id> returns the
# header alone, and the loop terminates the way it was written to.  With that
# in place the framework's own wifi-service.jar works untouched, so the jar in
# this build is the unmodified one the build system produced, and nothing
# anywhere writes wpa_supplicant.conf or networkHistory.txt.
#
# What that removed: kcwpa (the 4.4 supplicant wrapper), the 4.4 blob itself,
# /vendor/lib/kcssl, kcwifi2.sh / kcwifi3.sh / kcwifiwd.sh (all three rewrote
# the conf file behind the framework's back), kcwpad, and the jar patch.  What
# stayed: kcwifi, which is what loads the prima driver in the first place.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The blobs themselves: they were linked against 4.4's bionic/libcutils/
# libmedia, which exported symbols 7.1 dropped (ioprio_set, list_add_tail, the
# two-argument AudioTrack::write, CameraParameters::KEY_BLINK_DETECT, ...).
# Every consumer died at dlopen with "cannot locate symbol", which is why
# rmt_storage (no modem EFS -> unknown baseband), audio.primary (no sound),
# camera.msm8226 (no camera app) and bluetooth.default never worked.
# /vendor/lib/libkccabi.so back-fills all of them at once, so it is preloaded
# into everything init spawns from here on.  It is built with -nostdlib: no
# NEEDED entries, no constructors, nothing that can fail.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# kyl23: 7.1 framework on top of a 4.4 vendor blob set.
#
# The vendor blobs were linked against 4.4's bionic/libcutils/libmedia, which
# exported symbols 7.1 dropped (ioprio_set, list_add_tail, the two-argument
# AudioTrack::write, CameraParameters::KEY_BLINK_DETECT, ...).  Every consumer
# died at dlopen with "cannot locate symbol", which is why rmt_storage (no
# modem EFS -> unknown baseband), audio.primary (no sound), camera.msm8226 (no
# camera app) and bluetooth.default never worked.  /vendor/lib/libkccabi.so
# back-fills all of them at once, so it is preloaded into everything init
# spawns from here on.  It is built with -nostdlib: no NEEDED entries, no
# constructors, nothing that can fail.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

on boot
    setprop wifi.interface wlan0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Modem remote filesystem.  rfs_access serves the modem's EFS requests
# (modemst1/2 + fsg).  Without it the firmware stalls during init and its own
# watchdog resets it, and Android shows an unknown baseband.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
service rfs_access /vendor/bin/rfs_access
    class core
    user system
    group system net_raw

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WiFi.
#
# prima is a "satellite" driver: it cannot bring wlan0 up until wcnss_service
# has fed WCNSS_cfg.dat to the chip over /dev/wcnss_wlan.  The load itself has
# to go through init_module(2), because 7.1's libhardware_legacy (and toybox
# insmod) use finit_module(2), which arrived in Linux 3.8 and this kernel is
# 3.4.  libkccabi makes finit_module() report success; kcwifi does the real
# load and retries until wlan0 exists, so a chip that comes up late still ends
# up working instead of leaving wifi dead for the session.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
service kcwifi /vendor/bin/kcwifi
    class core
    user root
    group root system wifi net_raw

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# fix33: WiFi reconnect and the Bluetooth crash loop.
#
# After an off/on cycle the supplicant is a fresh process that, unlike at boot,
# does not select the saved network by itself -- and the framework cannot ask
# it to, because it never adopted that network (it rebuilds it with a made-up
# netId, does not find it in its own list, and drops it:
# "configuration found for missing network, nid=-1616136200, ignored,
# networks.size=1"; -1616136200 is "K2"WPA_PSK.hashCode()).  So the phone scans
# and scans and never joins.  kcwpad is the missing peer: it talks wpa_ctrl on
# /data/misc/wifi/sockets/wlan0 -- the build has no wpa_cli -- and sends
# RECONNECT when the supplicant has been sitting disconnected with an enabled
# network.  It also keeps the conf file itself from being eaten, and disables
# the com.android.bluetooth package, whose 4.4 vendor stack cannot survive the
# 7.1 framework and otherwise SIGSEGVs every 0.7 s.
#
# fix34 turned it from "started, logged four lines, then silence" into a
# daemon that says what it sees on every poll (state, scan result count,
# network list, and the exact step that failed if the ctrl socket is not
# reachable), because last round it was impossible to tell "there was nothing
# to do" from "it gave up".  On top of the kick it now:
#   * strips bssid= from the saved block -- a bssid pin plus a firmware reload
#     (the framework reloads the radio on every enable) means the supplicant
#     scans but can never select a BSS;
#   * pushes the saved network back into a live supplicant that has none
#     (ADD_NETWORK/SET_NETWORK/ENABLE_NETWORK), which is the case that ends in
#     "input the password again";
#   * creates /dev/kmsg when the image has none, so kernel-log breadcrumbs
#     exist even if /cache is truncated.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# fix40: kcwpad is retired.
#
# It did the right thing in the wrong place.  Its job was to put the saved
# network back when the supplicant came up without one, but it wrote the conf
# *file*, and a running supplicant never re-reads that file -- so after an
# off/on cycle it restored the file while the live process kept an empty
# configuration, the framework kept aiming SET_NETWORK at network id 0, the
# blob answered FAIL, CMD_AUTO_CONNECT gave up with "couldn't update the
# config, got null config", and the phone scanned forever without joining.
# Its own log said as much: "ctrl open failed (connect:Connection refused)".
#
# kcwd talks to the running process instead, over the interface control socket
# /data/misc/wifi/sockets/wlan0 (4.4 style, bare commands) and, failing that,
# the abstract name wpa_wlan0 that the 7.1 framework uses.  When LIST_NETWORKS
# comes back with no rows it recreates the saved network with ADD_NETWORK /
# SET_NETWORK / ENABLE_NETWORK / SAVE_CONFIG, which repairs the live process
# *and* the file in one step.  It stays out of the way otherwise: it only acts
# when the supplicant has no networks at all, so it never fights the framework
# over a network the user actually has, and it keeps /data/misc/wifi/kc_wpa.gold
# as the last known good copy.
#
# service kcwpad /vendor/bin/kcwpad
#     class late_start
#     user root
#     group root system wifi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# fix43: kcwd is gone, and so is the 4.4 supplicant.
#
# Everything above this line was written to keep a 4.4 wpa_supplicant blob
# alive under a 7.1 framework: kcwpad talked to it, kcwd repaired its config
# file, the framework's jar was patched so LIST_NETWORKS would be understood.
# The real fix is the one the device should have shipped with -- /system/bin
# wpa_supplicant, built from cm-14.1's own external/wpa_supplicant_8, which
# speaks the ctrl_iface dialect the framework expects.  With it in place the
# network ids agree, SAVE_CONFIG stores a real config, and the saved network
# survives an off/on cycle without any help.
#
# So: no daemon, no file repair, no jar patch.  See the p2p_supplicant
# service below for the one line that changed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WiFi, part two: the framework's half.
#
# fix29 proved the config side was fine -- /data/misc/wifi/wpa_supplicant.conf
# carries psk= and update_config=1 -- and the phone still refused to reconnect
# after a single off/on cycle.  logcat says why, twice:
#
#     WifiMonitor: killSupplicant p2ptrue init.svc.wpa_supplicant=unknown
#     WifiStateMachine: Failed to load driver
#
# "Failed to load driver" is 7.1's libhardware_legacy/wifi.c asking for the
# module: is_wifi_driver_loaded() looks at /proc/modules *and* at the property
# wlan.driver.status, and when that check misses it calls insmod(), which uses
# finit_module(2).  This kernel is 3.4; finit_module does not exist, so insmod
# fails in two milliseconds and the whole wifi stack stops before it ever
# reaches the supplicant -- no supplicant, no config, no saved networks, which
# is the "it forgot my password again" screen.
#
# wlan0 is already up at that point: wlan_up.sh loaded prima with
# init_module(2) at 10 s and set wlan.driver.status=ok.  The framework is
# arguing about a module it does not need to load.
#
# So wifi_load_driver() and wifi_unload_driver() in /lib/libhardware_legacy.so
# are patched to "movs r0,#0 ; bx lr" -- report success, touch nothing.  The
# interface then comes up once per boot (wlan_up.sh) and stays up for the life
# of the boot: turning wifi off stops the supplicant, turning it on starts a new
# one against a live wlan0, and the saved network is there every time.
#
# kcwifiwd is the insurance: if wlan0 ever does disappear, reload it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WiFi, part three: keep the saved network on disk for the whole session.
#
# fix31 made the file correct at boot and the phone now joins K2 by itself
# after a cold start -- but not after a WiFi off/on cycle, and the password has
# to be typed again every time.  The dump finally shows why, and it is not the
# supplicant's fault:
#
#     WifiConfigManager: Loading config and enabling all networks
#     WifiConfigManager: configuration found for missing network,
#                        nid=-1616136200, ignored, networks.size=1
#
# -1616136200 is Java's "K2"WPA_PSK.hashCode(): the framework rebuilds the
# network from the file, gets a made-up netId instead of the supplicant's 0,
# throws it away, and therefore holds *zero* configured networks for the whole
# boot.  WiFi still works at boot because the supplicant joins a network it
# already has without asking the framework.
#
# Turning WiFi off is where that empty list becomes data loss.
# WifiConfigStore.saveConfig() begins by calling removeNetwork() for every
# netId the supplicant has and the framework does not know about, then saves.
# With an empty list that is "remove the only network, then save the empty
# result" -- the psk is deleted from /data/misc/wifi/wpa_supplicant.conf, not
# merely forgotten.  Turning WiFi back on starts a supplicant with no networks
# at all, Settings asks for the password (ID: -1, hasEverConnected: false), and
# each entry deposits one more disabled=1 copy of K2 -- the file grew from 1
# block to 4 in a single session.
#
# kcwifi3 keeps a copy of the healthy file (one block, psk=, no disabled=1) in
# /data/misc/wifi/kc_wpa.gold and in /cache, and puts it back the moment the
# file loses it, which is while WiFi is still off -- so the next supplicant
# start is byte for byte the one that auto-joins after a reboot.  If the file
# comes back with duplicates, it collapses them per SSID.  After a repair it
# sends SIGHUP to the supplicant, which it treats as "reconfigure": the repair
# also takes effect when WiFi is already on, without wpa_cli (there is none on
# this build).  See kcwifi3.sh.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bluetooth: stop the crash loop.
#
# The kernel's user-fault reporter finally caught the crash this time:
#
#     USRFLT BluetoothAdapte/3667 addr=0000003c fsr=00005
#     USRFLT pc ... bluetooth.default.so off=000343f2 (ldr r2,[r4,#0x3c], r4=0)
#
# bluetooth.default.so dereferences a NULL callback struct 0x3c bytes in -- the
# 7.1 framework hands the 4.4 vendor stack a bt_callbacks_t of the wrong shape,
# the guard before it passes, and the stack walks off the end of the struct.
# Same root as everything else on this port: the blobs predate the framework.
# It crashes, the framework restarts AdapterService, it crashes again: 100 times
# in four minutes, each one spawning debuggerd, and that storm is why a 1 MB
# logcat only held 75 seconds and why the UI stutters.
#
# btif_init_bluetooth is patched to return BT_STATUS_NOT_READY immediately, so
# the stack never gets far enough to walk off the struct: AdapterService sees
# initNative fail and stays off, quietly, with no crash and no restart loop.
# Bluetooth still cannot be turned on -- the WCNSS BT core never comes up on
# this port -- but it no longer costs the rest of the phone anything.
# Reverting this one 4-byte patch brings the crashes back.
#
# fix31: that was only half of it.  The stack still crashed every 1.3 s, and
# the kernel's fault reporter said why -- JNI wrappers hand the HAL's
# bt_status_t back as if it were a pointer:
#
#     libbluetooth_jni.so off=00015b82, 00016416, and then 0001481c
#
# Each is an `ldr r1,[r0,#4]` / `blx r1` pair that runs when the status is not
# zero, i.e. it dereferences 2 (BT_STATUS_NOT_READY) as an address and faults
# at 0x6.  fix26 patched the first one at a wrong offset, fix27 patched the
# second and the crash moved back to the first, fix31 patched both and the
# crash moved to a third: whack-a-mole, because the whole file is built from
# one template.
#
# fix32 stops playing that game.  The template is
#
#     ldr rX,[iface,#0x58] ; blx rX ; cmp r0,#0 ; str r0,[global] ; beq err
#     ldr rY,[r0,#4] ; blx rY
#
# -- 7.1's slot 0x58 is get_profile_interface, but the 4.4 vendor library has a
# different table layout, so 0x58 is a function that returns a bt_status_t and
# the JNI dereferences 2 as if it were a profile struct.  All eleven copies of
# that template are patched (the beq before each dereference becomes an
# unconditional branch), so the status is logged and the call returns instead
# of faulting.  Even with a correct offset the seven-year-old profile structs
# behind it would not survive contact with the 7.1 callbacks, so Bluetooth
# stays off -- but it stays off quietly: the session before this one restarted
# AdapterService ~380 times in seven minutes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

service kcssr /vendor/bin/kcssr.sh
    class core
    user root
    group root system
    oneshot

# The ADSP never loads on this port, and nothing that needs audio works without
# it: the SLIMBUS controller is NGSLIM (it drives the bus through the ADSP), so
# no ADSP means no tapan codec, no sound card, no audio calibration, no sensor
# daemon and no DSP camera post-processing.  See kcadsp.sh.
service kcadsp /vendor/bin/kcadsp.sh
    class core
    user root
    group root system
    oneshot

# The first attempt runs at 5 s, before audioserver, because the audio HAL
# blocks in adev_open() until the sound card exists.  If the ADSP was not ready
# that early, try again once the framework is up: a late boot is still better
# than a phone without sound.
on property:sys.boot_completed=1
    start kcadsp

# The only supplicant in this build.  It pages its network list correctly, so
# the framework's unmodified wifi-service.jar works with it -- see the header.
# -d stays on: it is the only reason the daemon's own view of a command is
# visible at all, and it is what turned this bug from a guess into two lines of
# log.  It costs a few kilobytes in /data/misc/wifi.
service p2p_supplicant /system/bin/wpa_supplicant \
    -ip2p0 -Dnl80211 -c/data/misc/wifi/p2p_supplicant.conf \
    -I/system/etc/wifi/p2p_supplicant_overlay.conf -N \
    -iwlan0 -Dnl80211 -c/data/misc/wifi/wpa_supplicant.conf \
    -I/system/etc/wifi/wpa_supplicant_overlay.conf \
    -O/data/misc/wifi/sockets -puse_p2p_group_interface=1 \
    -e/data/misc/wifi/entropy.bin -g@android:wpa_wlan0 -d
    class main
    socket wpa_wlan0 dgram 660 wifi wifi
    disabled
    oneshot

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Capacitive key backlight.  7.1 does not time LIGHT_ID_BUTTONS out by itself
# and the framework never even asks for it at a key press, so a tiny daemon
# owns the LED:
#   * lights it on any EV_KEY down (logging what the kernel read back),
#   * kills it after the timeout, or immediately when the panel blanks,
#   * follows Settings -> Buttons -> Backlight, i.e. CMSettings.Secure
#     "button_brightness" (0 = illumination off) and "button_backlight_timeout"
#     (milliseconds).
#
# The two values are read by the daemon itself, straight out of the provider's
# SQLite file: it walks the b-tree read-only, with no sqlite3 binary, no locks
# and no child process.  Every earlier attempt used a shell loop with sqlite3,
# and each one failed at a different layer -- wrong key name (fix13), missing
# /xbin/sqlite3 (fix15), and finally a script that logged "started" and then
# never logged anything again (fix16/fix17), which left the daemon on its
# compiled-in 255/5000 defaults no matter what the page said.  Parsing the file
# in-process removes that whole class of failure.
#
# State changes go to /cache/kclog.txt (mirrored to /data/misc and the card) and
# every decision is also written to the kernel log as KCLED -- that second path
# is the important one: the cache partition is 458 MB and a dump only ever
# returns its first 20 MB, while the kernel log lands on the 20 MB "log"
# partition, which dumps in well under a minute.  The daemon also prints the
# physical offset of each of its log files with FIEMAP, so "the log is missing"
# can never again be confused with "the log was written past the dump window".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
service kcbtnled /vendor/bin/kcbtnled
    class core
    user root
    group root system input

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /dev/fuse does not exist: the kernel has FUSE built in (no uevent) and the
# ramdisk ueventd.rc has no static entry for it.  Create it before vold tries
# to bring up /storage/emulated/0, otherwise /sdcard stays empty forever.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
on post-fs-data
    # This file is parsed from /system/etc/init, which init only reads once
    # /system is mounted -- that happens *after* the "init" trigger, so an
    # on-init export here would never run.  post-fs-data is the last trigger
    # before "boot", which is where class_start core/main (zygote, mediaserver,
    # audioserver) happens, so everything spawned from here on inherits it.
    export LD_PRELOAD /vendor/lib/libkccabi.so

    # A NULL deref inside cfg80211 must not take the whole phone down.  The
    # kernel has panic_on_oops=1 (Kyocera set it so ramdump would catch every
    # oops); with it every prima teardown turns into a reboot loop.  Let the
    # oops kill the offending thread instead, and keep the log we need.
    write /proc/sys/kernel/panic_on_oops 0

    # PRODUCT_COPY_FILES in the device makefile installs everything 0644, so
    # the helpers arrive without the execute bit on a ROM built from source
    # (the images shipped during bring-up were built by hand and already had
    # it).  Set it here, before any of them is exec'd or started below.  On a
    # hand-built image this is a no-op.
    chmod 0755 /vendor/bin/kcwifi /vendor/bin/kcboot /vendor/bin/kcbtnled
    chmod 0755 /vendor/bin/kcfuse.sh /vendor/bin/kcssr.sh /vendor/bin/kcadsp.sh
    chmod 0755 /vendor/bin/kctail.sh /vendor/bin/kcshut.sh /vendor/bin/kchalt.sh
    chmod 0755 /vendor/bin/kcstate.sh /vendor/bin/kcstate-boot.sh
    chmod 0755 /vendor/bin/kcstate-delay.sh /vendor/bin/kc_bootanim.sh
    chmod 0755 /vendor/bin/kccam.sh /system/etc/kcfix.sh
    exec -- /vendor/bin/kcfuse.sh
    exec -- /vendor/bin/kcboot mknod
    mkdir /data/misc/wifi 0770 wifi wifi
    # QMI transport nodes.  This kernel has CONFIG_DEVTMPFS=n, so they come
    # from ueventd.qcom.rc with the 4.4 ownership; qmuxd runs as radio and
    # fails with "qmi_qmux_if_pwr_up_init failed rc=-1" unless it can open
    # them, which leaves every RIL request unanswered (baseband "unknown").
    chown radio radio /dev/qmi0
    chown radio radio /dev/qmi1
    chown radio radio /dev/qmi2
    chmod 0660 /dev/qmi0
    chmod 0660 /dev/qmi1
    chmod 0660 /dev/qmi2
    chown radio radio /dev/smdcntl0
    chown radio radio /dev/smdcntl1
    chmod 0660 /dev/smdcntl0
    chmod 0660 /dev/smdcntl1
    mkdir /data/misc/wifi/sockets 0770 wifi wifi
    mkdir /data/misc/audio 0770 audio audio

    # Nothing in this build touches wpa_supplicant.conf or networkHistory.txt.
    # A network written by a script is a network the framework cannot manage,
    # and the saved list is the framework's data, not ours.
    #
    # What is left in kcfix.sh is one system setting:
    #   - captive_portal_mode=IGNORE, because on 7.1 a single failed internet
    #     probe permanently disables a network (threshold 1, timeout
    #     MAX_VALUE) and the gstatic probe cannot succeed from a CN network.
    exec -- /system/bin/sh /system/etc/kcfix.sh

    # The jar below was put back to the build system's own copy (see the
    # header).  Its dex checksum differs from the one ART cached, so it would
    # be recompiled anyway; removing the stale oat makes that certain rather
    # than likely, and costs one line.
    exec -- /system/bin/rm -f /data/dalvik-cache/arm/system@framework@wifi-service.jar@classes.dex
    exec -- /system/bin/rm -f /data/dalvik-cache/system@framework@wifi-service.jar@classes.dex

    # Both of these are declared as services further up.  They have to be
    # started by name: this file is parsed at 4.3 s, long after init has already
    # run class_start core, so a plain "class core" service added here would
    # never run at all -- which is exactly what happened to kcssr in fix23.
    start kcssr
    start kcadsp

    # /cache is where every one of these scripts leaves its transcript, and
    # the bluetooth process (uid 1002) needs to be able to write next to it.
    chmod 0777 /cache

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Health report for the subsystems that are still broken (key-light settings,
# WiFi, audio, camera, baseband).  Runs at boot_completed and again two minutes
# later, because the supplicant / audio HAL only fail once the framework is up.
# Writing to /cache alone is not enough: the user's dump of that partition is
# truncated, so every line is also pushed to /dev/kmsg ("KCST ...") and
# therefore survives on the log partition.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runs on a timer from late_start; sys.boot_completed is far too late here.
# Watches for the shutdown and records how far the kernel got.  See kcshut.sh.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# fix28: keep the tail of logcat, not the head.
#
# kcrec stops at 1 MB (~75 s into the boot); the settings freeze happened at
# 74 s and its aftermath -- the watchdog kill of system_server, the audioserver
# respawn loop -- was past the end of the file.  kctail keeps only the tags
# that explain a hang, in five rotating 192 KB files, so the last ten minutes
# before a freeze always survive.  See kctail.sh.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
service kctail /vendor/bin/kctail.sh
    class late_start
    user root
    group root system log

service kcshut /vendor/bin/kcshut.sh
    class late_start
    user root
    group root system
    oneshot

# Mirrors the camera open/preview transitions into the kernel log, which is the
# only channel a dump always carries in full.  See kccam.sh.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reboot that finishes.
#
# The framework sets sys.shutdown.requested the moment the user picks Reboot or
# Power off; sys.powerctl follows a few seconds later, once userspace is done.
# Up to fix28 the kernel then took over and sat there for eight minutes waiting
# for a modem that is permanently resetting itself.  kchalt.sh waits for
# sys.powerctl and then pulls the sysrq lever, which is the same shortcut a long
# power-press takes: emergency_restart(), with no subsystem stop to wait for.
# See kchalt.sh.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Declared as a service, not run with "exec": init blocks until an exec'd
# child exits, and that would stall init's property socket -- including the
# sys.powerctl the framework is about to write, which is exactly what kchalt
# has to see.  As a service it runs beside init and the property goes through.
service kchalt /vendor/bin/kchalt.sh
    class late_start
    user root
    group root system
    oneshot
    disabled

on property:sys.shutdown.requested=*
    start kchalt

service kcstate /vendor/bin/kcstate-boot.sh
    class late_start
    user root
    group root system
    oneshot

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Board diagnostics.  kcboot is a static binary because the shell probe that
# came before it started but never produced a single line: this kernel has
# CONFIG_DEVTMPFS=n, the stock ueventd.rc has no /dev/kmsg, and every write
# went to /dev/null.  kcboot creates /dev/kmsg itself *and* writes the same
# transcript to /cache/kc_diag.txt, because the kernel log capture drops
# bursts.  Runs twice: once at boot and once when the framework says it is
# done, so the LED/UIO/storage state can be compared.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
service kcdiag /vendor/bin/kcboot diag
    class core
    user root
    group root system
    oneshot

service kcdiag2 /vendor/bin/kcboot diag2
    class late_start
    user root
    group root system
    oneshot
    disabled

# fix47: the kcprobe/wpaq pair is gone.  It existed to answer one question --
# what does the daemon reply to SET_NETWORK -- and it answered it.  With Wi-Fi
# working and nothing writing the config, there is nothing left for it to watch,
# and a probe that runs on every boot is just another thing that can fail.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
